# Flutter — Dropdown

## 1. What is a Dropdown?

A dropdown lets the user **select one option from multiple choices**.

Example:

```text
Country
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ India                 ▼ │
└─────────────────────────┘
```

When clicked:

```text
India
USA
UK
Japan
Germany
```

---

# 2. `DropdownButton`

Basic example:

```dart id="x8q3fz"
String selectedCountry = "India";

DropdownButton<String>(
  value: selectedCountry,

  items: [
    DropdownMenuItem(
      value: "India",
      child: Text("India"),
    ),

    DropdownMenuItem(
      value: "USA",
      child: Text("USA"),
    ),

    DropdownMenuItem(
      value: "UK",
      child: Text("UK"),
    ),
  ],

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      selectedCountry = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

---

# 3. Why `setState()`?

Suppose the user selects:

```text
India → USA
```

We need to update the value displayed by the dropdown.

```dart id="f0shk8"
setState(() {
  selectedCountry = value!;
});
```

`setState()` tells Flutter:

> "The data changed. Rebuild this part of the UI."

---

# 4. Dynamic Dropdown

Instead of manually writing every option, keep the choices in a list.

```dart id="v7g8d1"
List<String> countries = [
  "India",
  "USA",
  "UK",
  "Japan",
  "Germany",
];
```

Then:

```dart id="qf5q2e"
String selectedCountry = "India";
```

Build the dropdown dynamically:

```dart id="p3h7w9"
DropdownButton<String>(
  value: selectedCountry,

  items: countries.map((country) {

    return DropdownMenuItem(
      value: country,
      child: Text(country),
    );

  }).toList(),

  onChanged: (value) {

    setState(() {
      selectedCountry = value!;
    });

  },
)
```

The important part is:

```dart id="gq6w7k"
countries.map(...)
```

It creates a dropdown item for every country in the list.

---

# 5. `DropdownButtonFormField`

When the dropdown is part of a **Form**, use:

```dart id="9h7b8m"
DropdownButtonFormField<String>(
  decoration: InputDecoration(
    labelText: "Country",
    border: OutlineInputBorder(),
  ),

  initialValue: selectedCountry,

  items: countries.map((country) {

    return DropdownMenuItem(
      value: country,
      child: Text(country),
    );

  }).toList(),

  onChanged: (value) {

    setState(() {
      selectedCountry = value!;
    });

  },
)
```

This gives the dropdown the same form-friendly appearance as `TextFormField`.

---

# 6. Dropdown + Form Validation

You can also validate it:

```dart id="prg5cy"
DropdownButtonFormField<String>(
  decoration: InputDecoration(
    labelText: "Country",
    border: OutlineInputBorder(),
  ),

  items: countries.map((country) {

    return DropdownMenuItem(
      value: country,
      child: Text(country),
    );

  }).toList(),

  onChanged: (value) {
    selectedCountry = value;
  },

  validator: (value) {

    if (value == null) {
      return "Please select a country";
    }

    return null;
  },
)
```

So your form can contain:

```text id="c7i7e3"
Form
 │
 ├── TextFormField
 │
 ├── TextFormField
 │
 ├── DropdownButtonFormField
 │
 └── Submit Button
```

---

# 7. Dropdown with Objects

In real apps, your data often contains more than just a string.

For example:

```dart id="3pj4yx"
List<Map<String, String>> countries = [
  {
    "name": "India",
    "code": "+91",
  },
  {
    "name": "USA",
    "code": "+1",
  },
  {
    "name": "UK",
    "code": "+44",
  },
];
```

You can display the name:

```dart id="5y2w1h"
DropdownButton<Map<String, String>>(
  value: selectedCountry,

  items: countries.map((country) {

    return DropdownMenuItem(
      value: country,
      child: Text(
        "${country["name"]} (${country["code"]})",
      ),
    );

  }).toList(),

  onChanged: (value) {
    setState(() {
      selectedCountry = value!;
    });
  },
)
```

This pattern is useful for things like:

```text
Country → name + code + flag
Currency → name + symbol + code
Category → name + id
```

---

# 8. Important Difference

### `DropdownButton`

Use when you simply need a dropdown.

```dart
DropdownButton(...)
```

### `DropdownButtonFormField`

Use when the dropdown is part of a form and you want things like:

```text
validation
label
border
error message
```

For forms, remember:

**`DropdownButtonFormField`**

---

# 9. Common Real-World Uses

Dropdowns are commonly used for:

* Country selection
* State selection
* City selection
* Gender
* Category
* Currency
* Language
* Sort options
* Payment method
* Education level

---

# 10. Mental Model

```text id="8m4j5c"
List of options
      ↓
Dropdown
      ↓
User selects one
      ↓
onChanged()
      ↓
selected value changes
      ↓
setState()
      ↓
UI updates
```

## One-line revision

**A dropdown allows the user to select one option from a list; use `DropdownButton` for a normal dropdown and `DropdownButtonFormField` when it is part of a form.**
